From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Name the cast's sink after the receiver in words rather than in hex

Naming the sink after the receiver stopped a new entry being remembered for
every cast, which was the point of it, and left the name itself unreadable:
eight characters of a checksum that say nothing to anybody looking at a
sound panel or a state file.

Which matters more than it sounds. Finding one of these names written into a
session manager's remembered defaults is how this whole class of problem is
noticed, and the person finding it should be able to see which television it
belonged to rather than having to work out what was hashed. Three evenings
were spent here comparing hexadecimal strings that each stood for the same
living room television.

So the name is the receiver's name, with everything a sink name may not hold
turned into an underscore and runs of those collapsed. The television that
calls itself "[LG] webOS TV UN74006LB" becomes LG_webOS_TV_UN74006LB, which
is the same every time it is cast to and different from any other receiver,
exactly as the checksum was, and says what it is.

A receiver that announces nothing usable falls back to the identifier of the
cast. That is different every time, which is the thing this avoids, so it is
a last resort rather than a default.

diff -ruN a/src/nd-pulseaudio.c b/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
--- a/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
+++ b/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
@@ -66,11 +66,43 @@
 static gchar *
 nd_pulseaudio_sink_name (NdPulseaudio *self)
 {
-  g_autofree gchar *digest = g_compute_checksum_for_string (G_CHECKSUM_SHA256,
-                                                            self->name ? self->name : "",
-                                                            -1);
+  GString *readable = g_string_new (NULL);
+  const gchar *name = self->name ? self->name : "";
+  gboolean last_was_break = TRUE;
 
-  return g_strdup_printf (ND_PA_SINK "_%.8s", digest);
+  /* A sink name may hold letters, digits, dots, underscores and hyphens, and
+   * a receiver is called things like "[LG] webOS TV UN74006LB". Everything
+   * else becomes an underscore, runs of them collapse, and the ends are left
+   * bare, so the name stays something a person reads rather than decodes.
+   * Whoever finds one of these in a state file or a sound panel should be
+   * able to see which television it belonged to without being told. */
+  for (const gchar *c = name; *c; c++)
+    {
+      if (g_ascii_isalnum (*c) || *c == '.' || *c == '-')
+        {
+          g_string_append_c (readable, *c);
+          last_was_break = FALSE;
+        }
+      else if (!last_was_break)
+        {
+          g_string_append_c (readable, '_');
+          last_was_break = TRUE;
+        }
+    }
+
+  while (readable->len > 0 && readable->str[readable->len - 1] == '_')
+    g_string_truncate (readable, readable->len - 1);
+
+  /* A receiver that announces nothing usable still needs a name, and the
+   * cast's own identifier is the only thing left to make one from. It is
+   * different every time, which is the thing this function exists to avoid,
+   * so it is the last resort rather than the default. */
+  if (readable->len == 0)
+    g_string_append_printf (readable, "%.8s", self->uuid ? self->uuid : "unnamed");
+
+  g_autofree gchar *body = g_string_free (readable, FALSE);
+
+  return g_strdup_printf (ND_PA_SINK "_%s", body);
 }
 
 static void
